  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday Night through Saturday)\nIssued at 1132 PM CDT Sat Aug 1 2026\n\nUpper level ridging will expand back into the area by Monday and \nremain in place through much of the week, allowing temperatures to\nincrease back above average. Highs in the upper 90s/near 100 \ndegrees are forecast by mid week. Low level southerly flow will \nincrease during this time and dew points will gradually increase \nas well. This may lead to another period of dangerous heat mid to \nlate this week for portions of the area...namely the Tulsa metro \narea and the AR River Valley.\n\nThe forecast remains dry for the next few days, but low rain \npotential could return Wednesday onward as upper troughing \ndevelops across the northern plains and a cold front is pushed \ninto S KS / N OK. This may allow for isolated diurnal showers and \nstorms across NE OK and NW AR, especially in the higher terrain. \nA series of shallow shortwaves rotating through the central plains\ncould then bring slightly better rain and storm potential \nThursday and/or Friday, mainly north of I-40 in the vicinity of \nthe frontal boundary. As is typical for this time of year, locally\nheavy rainfall and gusty winds will be the main impacts \nassociated with any convection. Long range guidance continues to \nfavor above average temperatures and near to below average \nprecipitation through mid month.",
